 ## Background
-The method originates from the need to overcome the optimization latency of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting. GRM (Generalizable Reconstruction Method) utilizes existing multi-view generation models to train a pixel-aligned Gaussian representation for faithful 3D generation using a single feed-forward pass [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of generalizable reconstruction from images has evolved from traditional Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines to learning-based approaches. Early work like GRM (Generalizable Reconstruction Method) utilized existing multi-view generation models to train a pixel-aligned Gaussian representation for faithful 3D generation using a single feed-forward pass [S1, unofficial]. This family of methods addresses the computational inefficiency of per-scene optimization, which is the standard in 3D Gaussian Splatting. By learning priors over geometry and appearance, these methods enable real-time or near-real-time 3D reconstruction and novel view synthesis. The field has expanded to include specialized applications such as human avatar reconstruction (HFGaussian, RnD-Avatar), dynamic scene modeling (Deformation Field MLP), and semantic feature lifting (Occam's LGS, Feature Uplifting).
 
